NOTICE for base/ark_linearprobe

Ported code. main_linearprobing.py and utils.py in this directory are copies of the embedding linear probe of the Arizona State University project Ark (https://github.com/jlianglab/Ark,
Ark_Plus/Linearprobing), taken on 2026-10-10 from
    /Users/jaroddefilippo/code/CSE494-IP/Ark-main/Ark_Plus/Linearprobing/
and ported for the CSE 507 Ark+ container (base/), to run the lab's linear probe as shipped on the embeddings B2's extractor caches (CSE 494 IP, Step VIII). They are used under the license in
LICENSE, "License Text for ASU GitHub Project", for academic and other non-commercial research. Its section 5(c) asks for a prominent notice of changes and a description of them in a
NOTICE file: this is that file.

What was changed. utils.py is unchanged (byte-identical). Every change to main_linearprobing.py is marked by a comment that starts `# ported:` and says why, on the changed line, or on the
line that replaces a deleted one. `git diff --no-index` against the upstream file lists the changes; the vendoring guard in ../ark_finetune/test_ark_finetune.py does exactly that and fails
on a hunk without a marker. In short:
  * imports: package-relative (the name collides with the container's base/utils.py); `import pandas` is dropped (nothing uses it, the container image has none); the new dataset modules are imported.
  * options: get_args_parser takes an optional argv, so that the runner can build the options without sys.argv. What main() hard-codes becomes an option: --results_dir (the hard-coded
    /results), and for the new dataset modules --train_list, --test_list and --data_dir (the shipped ones hard-code /data/... paths in main()).
  * CPU only: --device is new and accepts "cpu" and nothing else (cpu_only, its argparse type, refuses the rest with a message). The shipped CUDA code is removed from main(): the
    CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES / "cuda" block (so --gpu now does nothing) and the DataParallel / .cuda() / cudnn block, which wrapped the model whenever CUDA was available and left the batches on
    the host, so it could not have run. The fit is a 1536 (ViT-B) or 2048 (ViT-L) x classes layer on the CPU.
  * DEVIATION from the shipped script, for reproducibility: --seed (default 0); run i is seeded with --seed + i (random, numpy, torch) before its data module is built.
  * dataset modules: main() builds ChestXray14, CheXpert, MIMIC and VinDrCXR modules (embedding_datasets.py, a new file) beside the two shipped ones, and passes them --batch_size and
    --num_workers (the shipped branches leave those to the module defaults, 512 and 4). They differ from the shipped modules in three named ways: the embeddings are in-memory arrays instead of
    one .npy file per image; the labels come from the official Finetuning dataset classes (ark_finetune/dataloader.py) as the Finetuning loop reads them (train sets LSR-Ones, MIMIC's test set
    "Ones"); and CheXpert is scored on its 5 competition findings, as the Finetuning branch does. Of the columns a dataset is scored on, the module keeps those with both a positive and a
    negative example among its test images (the shipped meanAUC skips an all-negative column silently, which shifts the per-class scores against the class names, and gives an all-positive one no defined AUC: sklearn raises or returns NaN, by version; a smoke cache of the first N images can make any column single-valued), records the
    others in dropped_columns, and refuses to be built if none is left. For that, test() takes an optional `columns` and scores only those, and main() passes it the module's test_columns.
  * main() keeps the best epoch's per-class scores of every run and returns one record per run (best epoch, the four mean metrics, the per-class scores, and the columns the module scored and
    dropped), for the results JSON.
Everything else is the script as shipped, and stays so on purpose: the float64 nn.Linear (model.double(), embedding.double()), torch.sigmoid + BCELoss, Adam with lr 1e-4, batch 512,
100 epochs, CosineAnnealingLR(T_max=1000, eta_min=1) stepped once per epoch (eta_min is above the base lr, so the lr RISES: 1e-4 at epoch 0, 0.00626 at epoch 50, 0.02409 at epoch 99),
the per-batch log, the metrics of utils.py, and the model selection: the epoch with the best TEST mAUC is the one kept and reported (the script has no validation split, and the early stopping
in it is commented out).

Not changed, though it does not apply here: the ChestDR and VinDrCXRAll modules, the LARS optimizer and the --multiclass path, and --few_shot (the new modules refuse it). The runner
(run_linearprobe.py) builds the options from fixed values and refuses any change to these settings; only --runs, --seed and the paths vary.
